# Client navigation

Full write-up: [docs/client-navigation.md](../../docs/client-navigation.md).

Header and docs taps use `next/link` plus the native App Shell. A first tap that “does nothing” is usually a React commit crash or a hydration race — not a reason to add an overlay or replace `<Link>` with `<a>`.

## Do

- Keep `SidebarProvider`, `SiteHeader`, and `DocsSidebar` mounted; hide with `hidden` / `contents`.
- `export const prefetch = "partial"` on the docs destination; leaf `loading.tsx` on the page that re-renders.
- `LinkPendingHint` (`useLinkStatus`) on the clicked control only.
- Update `theme-color` in place; render the header SVG atlas through React (`dangerouslySetInnerHTML`).
- `toggleSidebar` reads `isMobileViewport()` at tap time. Sidebar links call `setOpenMobile(false)`.
- Keep `usePathname()` out of the chrome Suspense fallback (`pathname=""` must not mount `DocsSidebar`).

## Don’t

- Full-viewport pending shells / `setTimeout` overlays (“150ms”).
- Tooltip wrappers on header or sidebar nav.
- `svg.innerHTML =` or `meta[name="theme-color"].remove()` on React-owned nodes.
- `usePathname()` / `useLinkStatus()` / `useSidebar()` in a prerender fallback.
- Global `partialPrefetching: true` without auditing every route.

If the URL changes and the old page stays, open the console for `removeChild` / `commitMutationEffects` before adding loading UI.

Canonical: `components/docs/app-chrome.tsx`, `lib/theme-color.ts`, `hooks/use-mobile.ts`.
